WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Speaker 1: We know that Republicans are going&nbsp;
to be taking over the House of Representatives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.280
at the start of 2023. And so that means that&nbsp;
for Democrats, there's not too many working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.080
days remaining on the congressional calendar&nbsp;
to get the things that you want to get passed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:18.360
passed. And it means that these next few weeks&nbsp;
we have to be all hands on deck to get these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:23.160
pieces of legislation through and get them&nbsp;
signed into law. Luckily, we'll still have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:28.020
the Senate after the start of 2023, but we're&nbsp;
not going have the House anymore. And really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.980
these opportunities where you have full control&nbsp;
of government, the House, Senate and presidency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.900
don't happen too often. And so you have to make&nbsp;
the best of the time where you have it all. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:42.120
have a few more weeks and so it's time to get&nbsp;
busy. My hope is that with this added sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.980
urgency, we'll be able to get the votes we need on&nbsp;
some of these things. And also, you don't have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:52.020
worry as much about some of these more vulnerable&nbsp;
senators and congresspeople because they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.700
have to worry about an election for another couple&nbsp;
of years at least. So maybe you'll have more votes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:02.040
post midterms than you had pre midterms. Chuck&nbsp;
Schumer is saying about this lame duck period, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:06.780
are going to try to have as productive a lame duck&nbsp;
session as possible. It's going to be heavy work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:12.060
long hours to try and get something done. Let's&nbsp;
hope he can deliver on this. I anticipate that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.320
he's going to run into many of the same issues&nbsp;
that we've had over these past couple of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.060
which is that you can't always get everyone on the&nbsp;
exact same page because you have slim majorities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.160
to work with in the House and Senate. Now, in&nbsp;
these next few weeks, there are some must do's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.720
in the agenda, some things that Democrats actually&nbsp;
absolutely have to get done. Like, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:36.900
they're going to need to pass legislation to keep&nbsp;
the government open and funded in order to avoid a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.940
government shutdown. They also have to pass the&nbsp;
National Defense Authorization Act. That has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:46.320
get passed so that the military can get funded.&nbsp;
And you better bet that we're going to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.660
find the money to do that, because that is always&nbsp;
a top priority in Washington. There's also this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:58.080
back and forth about raising the debt limit from&nbsp;
its current $31.4 trillion cap. Republicans may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:04.140
try to hold this hostage in exchange for some&nbsp;
concessions on spending cuts. To be honest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:09.540
the debt ceiling talk and the government shutdown&nbsp;
talk, it doesn't interest me that much because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:14.700
these things happen like every couple of years,&nbsp;
maybe even more frequently than that. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.080
always the same discussion. We always know that&nbsp;
they're going to do the right thing to keep the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.800
government open and so that we won't default&nbsp;
on our debt and all these different things. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.020
it's just this big charade that comes up. I'm more&nbsp;
focused on the policy items that Democrats can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:34.080
done in these next couple of weeks. Like, for one,&nbsp;
they still have to codify interracial marriage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.180
and same sex marriage. They had that preliminary&nbsp;
vote on the topic and 12 Republicans in the Senate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.560
crossed over and voted along with the Democrats.&nbsp;
But they still need to pass this thing one more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:49.500
time in the Senate. Hopefully that's a shoe in my&nbsp;
sense, is that it is in terms of other priorities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:56.700
They have to pass some legislation to clarify the&nbsp;
Electoral Count Act of 1887, which became a point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:03.000
of interest when Trump was trying to overturn the&nbsp;
2020 election results. Democrats want to clarify.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.400
The vice president's role is purely ceremonial&nbsp;
when they count the electoral votes. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.660
it was believed for many, many years to&nbsp;
be ceremonial. But then Trump decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:17.340
torpedo democracy and argued that Pence was&nbsp;
able to overturn the election in his favor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:23.520
And so Democrats want to make the text very much&nbsp;
clear on how you count electoral votes so that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.800
vice president can't just turn things over so&nbsp;
that they win. This is what Trump interpreted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.400
to be a vindication, by the way, because he was&nbsp;
trying to claim that the whole time Mike Pence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:38.400
had the authority to do this, and the fact that&nbsp;
Democrats are changing the law now means that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.960
was right, that Pence could have done this, and it&nbsp;
doesn't really make any sense. But anyway, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.920
trying to do that. They're also looking at getting&nbsp;
more aid approved for Ukraine, especially with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:53.100
expectation that Kevin McCarthy is not going to&nbsp;
be amenable to approving more aid funding for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:58.620
Ukraine once the Republicans take over. There's&nbsp;
contingents on the right, these noninterventionist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.360
types, also these people who think that we&nbsp;
shouldn't help Ukraine at all in their fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.160
against the Russian invasion for one reason or&nbsp;
another, because they're okay with Putin's regime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.960
they're not going to want to see aid going&nbsp;
towards Ukraine. So that's another thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.880
Democrats can try to do one last ditch effort on&nbsp;
that. They're also trying to get protections for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.920
Dreamers with these final few weeks undocumented&nbsp;
immigrants who are brought to the U.S. as minors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:28.740
even though they didn't make the decision on their&nbsp;
own to come here, they still have to deal with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:33.480
you know, potential deportation, potentially&nbsp;
going back to a country that they never knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.860
because they weren't born here in the United&nbsp;
States and aren't technically citizens. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:41.820
that's another thing that Biden should try to do&nbsp;
in these next few weeks. I think that personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:47.340
if you're going to go pedal to the metal on any of&nbsp;
these issues, it should probably be the dreamers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:52.500
because it's perhaps the most consequential.&nbsp;
Like the same sex marriage thing, an interracial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.580
marriage bill that'll probably get done pretty&nbsp;
easily. I'm also assuming we're going to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:01.200
to keep the government funded and, you know, raise&nbsp;
the. Debt ceiling and all of those procedural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.460
types of things. But in terms of what you can&nbsp;
actually do to make a material difference for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:11.460
public and how you can sort of increase Democratic&nbsp;
poll numbers and just do the most good with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:15.780
time that you have left. I think that protecting&nbsp;
the Dreamers should be a high priority. I wish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:19.920
that there could be more things on this list.&nbsp;
But, you know, we've known since the start of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.840
this Congress, the start of this presidency, that&nbsp;
we were not going to be able to get done all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:30.600
progressive wish list items that we wanted to get&nbsp;
accomplished done because we just were never going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:34.440
to have the votes. We weren't able to repeal the&nbsp;
filibuster and all this stuff. But that doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.760
mean that you can't grab all these low hanging&nbsp;
fruit. That's what we've been trying to do over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:42.720
these past couple of years, and hopefully with&nbsp;
these final weeks, we'll be able to do more of it.

